# Review is enforced by identity, not by convention (D260921.3-P).
#
# GitHub refuses to let an author approve their own pull request. That is the
# whole mechanism, and it only works once there are two identities:
#
#   @chris-egan   the owner
#   @sloporgAI    the machine account, the implementing lane
#
# Both are listed, and GitHub requires approval from at least ONE code owner
# who is not the author. So a PR raised by the machine lane needs the owner,
# and a PR raised by the owner needs the machine lane. Neither can wave its
# own work through, and neither can be blocked by having nobody to ask.
#
# Listing only one account would deadlock that account's own PRs permanently,
# which is a worse failure than no rule at all: it looks like enforcement and
# is actually a wall.
* @chris-egan @sloporgAI

# The rails and the CI that runs them belong to the owner alone (D260921.5-P).
#
# Withholding the Workflows permission was supposed to stop an agent disarming
# its own guard rails. It never did: the rail LOGIC is tools/hooks/checks.py,
# an ordinary repo file any agent can write, and a pull request is checked by
# its own version of it -- demonstrated by PR #9, whose rail F existed only on
# that branch and still fired. Permissions protected the four-line invoker and
# left the part that matters open, while blocking CI changes entirely.
#
# So the boundary moves to where it was really being held: review. Last match
# wins in CODEOWNERS, so these two override the line above and only the owner
# can approve a change to the rails or to the workflows that run them.
/.github/ @chris-egan
/tools/hooks/ @chris-egan
